/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*-------------------------- File compression.dox --------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_compression */
/** \defgroup theory_compression Theory of compression
 \ingroup area_compression

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/compression` realize the lossless
 compressors of chapters 11, Integer Coding, 12, Statistical Coding, 13,
 Dictionary-Based Compressors, and 14, Block-Sorting Compression, of
 Ferragina's book, beyond the book, which meets them only in the code of
 Huffman of its section 14.4, and the indexes that search a compressed text:
 the entropy against which every compressor is measured, the statistical codes
 (Huffman, arithmetic, asymmetric numeral systems, prediction by partial
 matching), the codes of the integers, the compressors of Lempel and Ziv, the
 transform of Burrows and Wheeler and the indexes built on it. This page
 collects what the headers take for granted: the definitions and the notation
 shared by all of them (see \ref theory_compression_notation), the limit that
 no code can beat and the codes that reach it (see
 \ref theory_compression_stat), the codes of the integers and their costs
 (see \ref theory_compression_int), and, for the dictionary compressors, the
 transform and the indexes, the property on which each one rests and the
 argument behind its bound (from \ref theory_compression_lz to
 \ref theory_compression_index). The pseudocode of each algorithm is instead
 on the page of its header, in the comment of its function, together with the
 chapter of Ferragina's book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_compression_notation Definitions and notation

 A text \f$ s = s[ 0 ] s[ 1 ] \cdots s[ n - 1 ] \f$ is a string of \f$ n \f$
 symbols of an alphabet \f$ \Sigma \f$ of \f$ \sigma \f$ symbols, ordered,
 usually the bytes; \f$ s[ i .. j ] \f$ is the substring from \f$ i \f$ to
 \f$ j \f$, and \f$ s[ i .. ] \f$ the suffix from \f$ i \f$. A
 <em>code</em> maps each symbol \f$ \sigma \f$ to a binary
 <em>codeword</em> \f$ c( \sigma ) \f$ of length \f$ \ell( \sigma ) \f$,
 and a text to the concatenation of the codewords of its symbols; the code
 is <em>prefix-free</em> if no codeword is a prefix of another one, so that
 the concatenation is decoded from left to right with no separator, each
 codeword being recognized when its last bit is read. A compressor is
 <em>lossless</em> when its decoder gives back the text exactly, which is the
 audit of every encoder of the area.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n_c \f$, \f$ P[ c ] \f$</td>
     <td>the occurrences of the symbol \f$ c \f$ in \f$ s \f$, and its
         probability, \f$ n_c / n \f$ for the empirical one</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ H_0 \f$, \f$ H_k \f$</td>
     <td>the entropy of order 0 and of order \f$ k \f$, in bits per symbol
         (see (1) and (2))</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \ell( c ) \f$, \f$ \bar\ell \f$</td>
     <td>the length of the codeword of \f$ c \f$, and the average
         \f$ \bar\ell = \sum_c P[ c ] \ell( c ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ B( x ) \f$, \f$ | B( x ) | \f$</td>
     <td>the binary code of the integer \f$ x \ge 1 \f$, and its length
         \f$ 1 + \lfloor \log_2 x \rfloor \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{SA} \f$</td>
     <td>the suffix array of \f$ s \$ \f$, the places of the suffixes in
         lexicographic order, \f$ \$ \f$ a sentinel smaller than every
         symbol [see <tt>SuffixArray.h</tt>]</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ F \f$, \f$ L \f$</td>
     <td>the first and the last column of the matrix of the sorted
         rotations of \f$ s \$ \f$; \f$ L \f$ is the transform of Burrows
         and Wheeler</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ C[ c ] \f$</td>
     <td>the number of the symbols of \f$ s \$ \f$ smaller than
         \f$ c \f$, i.e., the row of \f$ F \f$ where the rows that start
         with \f$ c \f$ begin</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{rank}_c( L , i ) \f$</td>
     <td>the occurrences of \f$ c \f$ in \f$ L[ 0 .. i - 1 ] \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ r \f$, \f$ z \f$</td>
     <td>the number of the runs of equal symbols of \f$ L \f$, and the
         number of the phrases of a parse of Lempel and Ziv</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ p \f$, \f$ \mathit{occ} \f$</td>
     <td>the length of a pattern \f$ P \f$, and the number of its
         occurrences in \f$ s \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 The <em>entropy of order 0</em> of a distribution \f$ P \f$ on
 \f$ \Sigma \f$, and the <em>empirical</em> one of \f$ s \f$, which takes
 \f$ P[ c ] = n_c / n \f$, are
 \f[
   H_0 = \sum_{c \in \Sigma} P[ c ] \log_2 \frac{1}{P[ c ]} , \tag{1}
 \f]
 the term of a symbol of probability 0 being 0; \f$ 0 \le H_0 \le \log_2
 \sigma \f$, with 0 when one symbol has all the probability and
 \f$ \log_2 \sigma \f$ when they are equally likely. A compressor that codes
 each symbol alone, whatever comes before it, cannot do better than
 \f$ n H_0 \f$ bits (see \ref theory_compression_stat), and the compressors
 that look at the context do better on the texts of a language, where a
 symbol is often all but fixed by the few before it. With \f$ w_s \f$ the
 string of the symbols that follow the occurrences of \f$ w \f$ in
 \f$ s \f$, in their order, the <em>entropy of order \f$ k \f$</em> of
 \f$ s \f$ is
 \f[
   H_k( s ) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{w \in \Sigma^k} |w_s| \, H_0( w_s ) , \tag{2}
 \f]
 the average entropy of the symbols in their contexts of \f$ k \f$ symbols;
 \f$ H_{k+1} \le H_k \le H_0 \f$, since conditioning on a longer context
 does not increase the entropy, and \f$ n H_k \f$ is the measure of the
 compressors of order \f$ k \f$ (`entropy()` of `Entropy.h` computes both).

 \section theory_compression_stat Statistical codes

 <em>Lemma 1 (Kraft and Shannon).</em> The lengths of a prefix-free code
 satisfy
 \f[
   \sum_{c \in \Sigma} 2^{-\ell( c )} \le 1 , \tag{3}
 \f]
 conversely any lengths that satisfy (3) are those of a prefix-free code,
 and the average length of a prefix-free code is at least \f$ H_0 \f$.

 Indeed, a codeword \f$ c( \sigma ) \f$ is a node of the complete binary tree
 of depth \f$ \max \ell \f$, and since no codeword is the ancestor of another
 one the \f$ 2^{\max \ell - \ell( \sigma )} \f$ leaves below the codewords are
 disjoint, which is (3) multiplied by \f$ 2^{\max \ell} \f$. Conversely, the
 canonical code below gives the codewords of given lengths in order, each
 the first free node of its level. Finally,
 \f$ H_0 - \bar\ell = \sum_c P[ c ] \log_2( 2^{-\ell( c )} / P[ c ] ) \le
 \log_2 \sum_c 2^{-\ell( c )} \le 0 \f$ by the concavity of the logarithm
 (the inequality of Jensen) and by (3); hence no code of the symbols one by
 one beats \f$ H_0 \f$ bits per symbol, and a code of lengths
 \f$ \ell( c ) = \log_2( 1 / P[ c ] ) \f$ would reach it, when these are
 integers.

 <em>Theorem 2 (Huffman).</em> The code of Huffman has the least average
 length among the prefix-free codes of the symbols, and
 \f[
   H_0 \le \bar\ell_H < H_0 + 1 . \tag{4}
 \f]

 The code is built by merging the two least probable symbols into one of
 their total probability, \f$ \sigma - 1 \f$ times, the codewords of the two
 being those of the merged symbol followed by 0 and by 1 (`huffman()` of
 `Huffman.h`, by a heap in \f$ O( \sigma \log \sigma ) \f$ time). An optimal
 code has the two least probable symbols as siblings at its deepest level,
 since swapping them with the deepest pair does not lengthen it; the merge
 then turns an optimal code of \f$ \sigma \f$ symbols into one of
 \f$ \sigma - 1 \f$ symbols whose average is less by the probability of the
 pair, which does not depend on the code, and by induction the code built
 is optimal. The lower bound of (4) is Lemma 1, and the upper one holds since
 the lengths \f$ \lceil \log_2( 1 / P[ c ] ) \rceil \f$ satisfy (3), give an
 average below \f$ H_0 + 1 \f$, and are no better than the optimum.

 Only the lengths matter: a <em>canonical</em> code assigns to the symbols
 of each length consecutive codewords, the first of length \f$ l \f$ being
 \f$ \mathit{fc}[ l ] = \lceil ( \mathit{fc}[ l + 1 ] + \mathit{num}[ l + 1 ]
 ) / 2 \rceil \f$ on \f$ l \f$ bits, with \f$ \mathit{fc}[ \max ] = 0 \f$
 (`CanonicalHuffman.h`). The codewords of length \f$ l \f$ are the values from
 \f$ \mathit{fc}[ l ] \f$ on, and the \f$ l \f$ first bits of a longer one are
 below \f$ \mathit{fc}[ l ] \f$; hence the decoder reads bits until the value
 \f$ v \f$ of the \f$ l \f$ bits read reaches \f$ \mathit{fc}[ l ] \f$, and
 the symbol is the \f$ ( v - \mathit{fc}[ l ] ) \f$-th of length \f$ l \f$,
 with the lengths alone as preamble. The fast decoders read \f$ W \f$ bits at
 once and find in a table of \f$ 2^W \f$ entries the symbols whose codewords
 fit in them, which needs codewords of at most \f$ W \f$ bits: the optimal
 code of lengths at most \f$ L \f$ is found by the package-merge of Larmore
 and Hirschberg in \f$ O( \sigma L ) \f$ time (`limitedHuffmanLengths()` of
 `HuffmanCoder.h`), and more simply, but not optimally, by halving the
 frequencies until the code of Huffman fits (`limitedLengths()`).

 A code of the symbols one by one loses up to one bit per symbol against
 \f$ H_0 \f$, which is much on a skewed distribution (\f$ H_0 \f$ near 0
 costs 1 bit per symbol anyway). The <em>arithmetic code</em> codes the whole
 text as one number: the symbols narrow an interval of \f$ [ 0 , 1 ) \f$ in
 turn, each to the part of its probability, and the code is a short binary
 fraction inside the last interval.

 <em>Theorem 3 (the arithmetic code).</em> With \f$ s_n = \prod_i P[ s[ i ]
 ] \f$ the size of the last interval \f$ [ l , l + s_n ) \f$, the first
 \f$ d = \lceil \log_2( 2 / s_n ) \rceil \f$ bits of \f$ l + s_n / 2 \f$ are
 a prefix-free code of \f$ s \f$ (among the texts of \f$ n \f$ symbols), and
 with the empirical probabilities
 \f[
   d \le 2 + \sum_{i} \log_2 \frac{1}{P[ s[ i ] ]} = 2 + n H_0 . \tag{5}
 \f]

 Truncating \f$ x = l + s_n / 2 \f$ to \f$ d \f$ bits loses less than
 \f$ 2^{-d} \le s_n / 2 \f$, so the fraction \f$ x' \f$ written and every
 fraction that begins with its bits lie in
 \f$ [ x' , x' + 2^{-d} ) \subseteq [ l , l + s_n ) \f$; since the intervals
 of two texts of \f$ n \f$ symbols are disjoint, no code is a prefix of
 another one. The sum in (5) is \f$ \sum_c n_c \log_2( n / n_c ) = n H_0 \f$.
 Hence the arithmetic code is within 2 bits of \f$ n H_0 \f$ for the whole
 text, against the \f$ n \f$ bits of (4), and it codes as well the
 probabilities that change at each symbol, as long as the decoder changes
 them in the same way (the adaptive model of `ArithmeticCoding.h`, and the
 contexts of `PPM.h`).

 The exact code needs numbers of \f$ d \f$ bits; the coder of Witten, Neal
 and Cleary (`RangeEncoder`) keeps the interval on \f$ w \f$ bits,
 \f$ [ L , H ) \subseteq [ 0 , M ) \f$, \f$ M = 2^w \f$, and emits a bit
 whenever the interval falls in one half of \f$ [ 0 , M ) \f$, doubling it.
 The interval of a symbol of counts \f$ [ a , b ) \f$ out of a total
 \f$ T \f$ is
 \f[
   [ L + \lfloor ( H - L ) a / T \rfloor , L + \lfloor ( H - L ) b / T
   \rfloor ) , \tag{6}
 \f]
 and an interval that straddles the middle within
 \f$ [ M / 4 , 3 M / 4 ) \f$ is doubled around the middle, the bit it owes
 being emitted after the next one, opposite to it. After these expansions
 \f$ H - L > M / 4 \f$, and with \f$ T \le M / 4 \f$ every interval of (6) is
 nonempty, since \f$ ( H - L ) ( b - a ) / T > 1 \f$; the rounding of (6)
 costs about \f$ 10^{-4} \f$ bits per symbol for \f$ w = 32 \f$.

 The <em>asymmetric numeral systems</em> of Duda reach the same compression
 with an integer state \f$ x \f$ in place of the interval. With the
 frequencies \f$ f_c \f$ scaled to a total \f$ M = 2^{12} \f$ and
 \f$ c_c \f$ their prefix sums, the symbol \f$ c \f$ moves the state to
 \f[
   C( c , x ) = M \lfloor x / f_c \rfloor + c_c + x \bmod f_c , \tag{7}
 \f]
 a bijection between the pairs \f$ ( c , x ) \f$ and the states, whose
 inverse reads \f$ c \f$ off \f$ x \bmod M \f$, which lies in
 \f$ [ c_c , c_c + f_c ) \f$. The state grows by the factor
 \f$ M / f_c \f$, i.e., by \f$ \log_2( M / f_c ) \f$ bits, the information
 of \f$ c \f$ under the scaled frequencies; the encoder keeps it in
 \f$ [ 2^{16} , 2^{32} ) \f$ by moving 16 bits to the stream before
 \f$ C \f$ whenever \f$ x \ge 2^{20} f_c \f$, so that \f$ C( c , x ) <
 2^{32} \f$. The decoder reads the stream backward, and the encoder therefore
 takes the symbols from the last; `Rans.h` computes (7) with a multiplication,
 and `Tans.h` tabulates it on \f$ 2^{11} \f$ states, a lookup per symbol.

 <table>
 <caption>The statistical coders of the area, on a text of \f$ n \f$
          symbols</caption>
 <tr><th>Coder</th><th>Bits</th><th>Time per symbol</th>
     <th>Note</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CanonicalHuffman</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ n \bar\ell_H < n ( H_0 + 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \ell( c ) ) \f$ to decode, a bit at a time</td>
     <td>any alphabet, the preamble the lengths</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>huffmanCompress()</tt></td>
     <td>as above, lengths at most 11</td>
     <td>up to 3 symbols per lookup to decode</td>
     <td>bytes, four streams</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>arithmeticEncode()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \le 2 + n H_0 \f$ (exact), about \f$ 10^{-4} n \f$ more on
         32 bits</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log \sigma ) \f$ adaptive (a tree of Fenwick)</td>
     <td>models that change at each symbol</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ransCompress()</tt>, <tt>tansCompress()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ n H_0 \f$ plus a fraction of a percent</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, a multiplication or a lookup</td>
     <td>static model, bytes</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ppmEncode()</tt></td>
     <td>approaches \f$ n H_K \f$ plus the escapes</td>
     <td>\f$ O( K ( K + \sigma_K ) \log n ) \f$</td>
     <td>contexts of up to \f$ K \f$ symbols</td></tr>
 </table>

 Prediction by partial matching (`PPM.h`) codes each symbol arithmetically in
 the longest context of at most \f$ K \f$ symbols that has seen it, escaping
 to the shorter contexts on the way. With the escape of the version C, a
 context seen \f$ m \f$ times with \f$ q \f$ distinct symbols gives
 \f$ c( \sigma ) / ( m + q ) \f$ to the symbol \f$ \sigma \f$ of count
 \f$ c( \sigma ) \f$ and \f$ q / ( m + q ) \f$ to the escape, and the
 symbols excluded by the longer contexts are left out of the shorter ones,
 since the decoder knows that the symbol is none of them. The compression
 grows with \f$ K \f$ up to 4 or 5 symbols on the texts of a language, beyond
 which the escapes cost more than the longer contexts gain.

 \section theory_compression_int Codes of the integers

 A code of the positive integers is optimal, by Lemma 1, for the
 distribution \f$ P[ x ] = 2^{-\ell( x )} \f$, and choosing a code is
 choosing the distribution of the data: the d-gaps of a sorted list (the
 differences of consecutive items, `dGaps()`) are small and skewed, and a
 code with short codewords for the small integers takes few bits on them.

 <table>
 <caption>The codes of <tt>IntegerCodes.h</tt> (chapter 11, Integer Coding,
          of Ferragina's book)</caption>
 <tr><th>Code</th><th>Codeword</th><th>Length</th>
     <th>Optimal for</th></tr>
 <tr><td>unary</td><td>\f$ 0^{x-1} 1 \f$</td><td>\f$ x \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ 2^{-x} \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \gamma \f$ (Elias)</td>
     <td>\f$ 0^{| B( x ) | - 1} B( x ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ 2 \lfloor \log_2 x \rfloor + 1 \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ 1 / ( 2 x^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \delta \f$ (Elias)</td>
     <td>\f$ \gamma( | B( x ) | ) B( x ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ 1 + 2 \lfloor \log_2 | B( x ) | \rfloor + | B( x ) | \f$</td>
     <td>about \f$ 1 / ( 2 x ( \log_2 x )^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Rice \f$ R_k \f$, Golomb</td>
     <td>\f$ U( q + 1 ) \f$ and the remainder</td>
     <td>\f$ \lfloor ( x - 1 ) / 2^k \rfloor + 1 + k \f$ for Rice</td>
     <td>\f$ ( 1 - p )^{x-1} p \f$, modulus about \f$ \ln 2 / p \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>variable byte</td><td>groups of 7 bits, a flag each</td>
     <td>\f$ 8 \lceil | B( x ) | / 7 \rceil \f$</td>
     <td>whole bytes, fast to decode</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ ( s , c ) \f$-dense</td><td>continuers, then a stopper</td>
     <td>\f$ k \f$ words for the \f$ s c^{k-1} \f$ integers of that
         length</td>
     <td>the \f$ s \f$ of <tt>denseOptimalS()</tt></td></tr>
 </table>

 Two codes adapt to the data rather than assume a distribution. The
 <em>interpolative</em> code of Moffat and Stuiver writes the middle item of
 a strictly increasing sequence in \f$ [ \mathit{low} , \mathit{hi} ] \f$
 between the bounds that the items around it leave, i.e., with
 \f$ x[ l .. r ] \f$ the items and \f$ m \f$ the middle one, in
 \f[
   \lceil \log_2( \mathit{hi} - \mathit{low} - r + l + 1 ) \rceil \tag{8}
 \f]
 bits, and then the two halves recursively; a run of consecutive integers
 leaves a single value to each item and costs no bit, which is why the code
 can beat any code of the items one by one on a clustered list. PForDelta
 codes a block in \f$ b \f$ bits per item, the few that do not fit being
 exceptions written apart, so that the decoder unpacks the block with no
 branch per item (`encodePForDelta()`); its engineered forms
 (`BinaryPacking.h`) pack 128 items in the 4 lanes of a register of 128 bits,
 4 items per shift and mask, and the patched one chooses the width of least
 cost from the counts of the widths of the block. The Stream VByte of
 Lemire, Kurz and Rupp moves the lengths of the variable-byte code out of the
 data, 2 bits per integer in control bytes, so that one shuffle of bytes
 decodes 4 integers (`StreamVByte.h`).

 None of these codes reads the \f$ i \f$-th item without decoding the ones
 before it. The directly addressable codes of Brisaboa, Ladra and Navarro
 (`DirectAccessCodes.h`) cut each integer into chunks, the level
 \f$ \ell \f$ holding the chunk \f$ \ell \f$ of the integers that reach it
 and a bit telling whether each one continues. The item \f$ i \f$ of a level
 is the item \f$ \mathrm{rank}_1( B_\ell , i ) \f$ of the next one, so that
 an access costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ per level of its integer; the widths of the
 levels that minimize the space, with \f$ n_s \f$ the integers of more than
 \f$ s \f$ bits and \f$ w \f$ the bits of the largest, satisfy
 \f[
   C( s ) = \min_{s < t \le w} \big( n_s ( t - s ) + [ t < w ] \, 1.25 \,
   n_s + C( t ) \big) , \qquad C( w ) = 0 , \tag{9}
 \f]
 since a level that starts at \f$ s \f$ costs \f$ t - s \f$ bits for each of
 the \f$ n_s \f$ integers that reach it, and one more (with a quarter for
 its rank) unless it is the last; (9) is solved from \f$ s = w - 1 \f$ down
 in \f$ O( w^2 ) \f$ time. The code of Elias and Fano, which also searches a
 sorted sequence in place, is in the area of the succinct structures [see
 `EliasFano.h`].

 \section theory_compression_lz Compressors of Lempel and Ziv

 The compressors of Lempel and Ziv need no model: they replace a substring with
 a reference to an earlier occurrence of it (`LempelZiv.h`, chapter 13,
 Dictionary-Based Compressors, of Ferragina's book). LZ77 parses the text into
 <em>phrases</em>, each the longest prefix of the rest of the text that starts
 in the window of the last \f$ w \f$ symbols, written as the triple of its
 distance, its length and the symbol after it; a copy may overlap the phrase
 itself, which the decoder handles by copying one symbol at a time. LZSS writes
 a copy or a literal, and finds the copies as gzip does, by chains of the
 places with the same hash of the next 3 symbols. LZ78 and LZW keep an explicit
 dictionary of phrases in a trie, each new phrase being an old one extended by
 one symbol. All of them are universal: on the texts of an ergodic source their
 bits per symbol tend to its entropy, and the code of the \f$ z \f$ phrases of
 LZ78 takes at most \f$ n H_k + O( n \log \log n / \log n ) \f$ bits for every
 fixed \f$ k \f$ (Kosaraju and Manzini), a term that vanishes slowly.

 The search of the window costs \f$ O( w \ell ) \f$ for a phrase of length
 \f$ \ell \f$, and \f$ O( n w ) \f$ in all; with no window the parse is found
 in \f$ O( n ) \f$ time (`lz77ParseLinear()` of `LZ77Linear.h`) by the
 following property.

 <em>Lemma 4 (Kärkkäinen, Kempa and Puglisi).</em> Let \f$ r \f$ be the rank
 of the suffix \f$ i \f$ in \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$, and \f$ \mathit{PSV}( i ) \f$
 and \f$ \mathit{NSV}( i ) \f$ the suffixes of the closest ranks before and
 after \f$ r \f$ that start before \f$ i \f$. The longest prefix of
 \f$ s[ i .. ] \f$ that starts at an earlier place has the length
 \f[
   \ell( i ) = \max\{ \mathrm{lcp}( i , \mathit{PSV}( i ) ) ,
   \mathrm{lcp}( i , \mathit{NSV}( i ) ) \} . \tag{10}
 \f]

 Indeed, the longest common prefix of two suffixes is the least of the
 longest common prefixes of the suffixes adjacent in \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$
 between them, and it can therefore only decrease as the rank moves away
 from \f$ r \f$; among the suffixes that start before \f$ i \f$, the closest
 on each side share the longest prefix with \f$ i \f$. The two values of
 every \f$ i \f$ come from one scan of \f$ \mathit{SA} \f$ with a stack, each
 place pushed and popped once, and the lengths of (10) are found by
 comparing symbols, at most \f$ \ell( i ) + 1 \f$ comparisons per candidate;
 since the phrases cover the text, these sum to \f$ O( n ) \f$, and the parse
 costs \f$ O( n ) \f$ with the suffix array of SA-IS.

 \section theory_compression_bwt The transform of Burrows and Wheeler

 The rows of the matrix \f$ M' \f$ are the \f$ n + 1 \f$ rotations of
 \f$ s \$ \f$ in sorted order, \f$ F \f$ its first column and \f$ L \f$ its
 last (`bwt()` of `BurrowsWheeler.h`, chapter 14, Block-Sorting Compression, of
 Ferragina's book). Since \f$ \$ \f$ is the smallest symbol and occurs once,
 sorting the rotations is sorting the suffixes, and
 \f[
   L[ i ] = s[ \mathit{SA}[ i ] - 1 ] , \tag{11}
 \f]
 the symbol before the \f$ i \f$-th smallest suffix (\f$ \$ \f$ for the
 suffix 0); hence the transform costs the \f$ O( n ) \f$ of the suffix array.
 The rows that start with a string \f$ w \f$ are contiguous, so the symbols
 that precede the occurrences of \f$ w \f$ are a substring of \f$ L \f$: on
 a text whose symbols depend on their context, \f$ L \f$ is made of long runs
 of few distinct symbols, which the move-to-front turns into small integers
 and the run-length code shortens. Coding \f$ L \f$ in this way takes at most
 \f$ ( 5 + \epsilon ) n H_k^* \f$ bits plus lower terms (Manzini), with
 \f$ H_k^* \f$ a variant of (2), and coding the pieces of \f$ L \f$ of each
 context of \f$ k \f$ symbols by a code of order 0 reaches \f$ n H_k \f$
 plus lower terms for every \f$ k \f$ at once (the boosting of Ferragina,
 Giancarlo, Manzini and Sciortino).

 <em>Lemma 5 (the LF-mapping).</em> The \f$ k \f$-th occurrence of a symbol
 \f$ c \f$ in \f$ L \f$ and the \f$ k \f$-th occurrence of \f$ c \f$ in
 \f$ F \f$ are the same symbol of \f$ s \f$; hence the row
 \f[
   \mathit{LF}( i ) = C[ L[ i ] ] + \mathrm{rank}_{L[ i ]}( L , i ) \tag{12}
 \f]
 is the one that starts with the symbol \f$ L[ i ] \f$, i.e., the rotation of
 the row \f$ i \f$ by one place to the right.

 Indeed, two rows \f$ i < j \f$ with \f$ L[ i ] = L[ j ] = c \f$, rotated by
 one place to the right, become \f$ c \f$ followed by the row \f$ i \f$ and
 \f$ c \f$ followed by the row \f$ j \f$, which compare as the rows did;
 hence the rows that start with \f$ c \f$ are in the order of the
 occurrences of \f$ c \f$ in \f$ L \f$, and they begin at the row
 \f$ C[ c ] \f$ of \f$ F \f$. Since \f$ L[ \mathit{LF}( i ) ] \f$ is the
 symbol before \f$ L[ i ] \f$ in \f$ s \f$, the inverse transform
 (`inverseBwt()`) starts from the row of \f$ \$ s \f$ and spells \f$ s \f$
 backward by \f$ n \f$ steps of (12), in \f$ O( n + \sigma ) \f$ time with
 the counts of \f$ L \f$ taken in one scan.

 \section theory_compression_index Compressed indexes

 The transform is also an index of the substrings of \f$ s \f$, which
 replaces both the text and its suffix array (Ferragina and Manzini).

 <em>Lemma 6 (the backward search).</em> If the rows prefixed by a string
 \f$ Q \f$ are \f$ [ \mathit{first} , \mathit{last} ) \f$, those prefixed by
 \f$ c Q \f$ are
 \f[
   [ C[ c ] + \mathrm{rank}_c( L , \mathit{first} ) ,
   C[ c ] + \mathrm{rank}_c( L , \mathit{last} ) ) . \tag{13}
 \f]

 In fact, a row is prefixed by \f$ c Q \f$ if and only if it is the image by
 (12) of a row prefixed by \f$ Q \f$ whose last symbol is \f$ c \f$, and the
 images of the occurrences of \f$ c \f$ in
 \f$ L[ \mathit{first} .. \mathit{last} - 1 ] \f$ are consecutive by
 Lemma 5. Hence `count( P )` applies (13) from the last symbol of \f$ P \f$
 to the first, \f$ p \f$ steps of two ranks each, and the occurrences are
 \f$ \mathit{last} - \mathit{first} \f$, with no access to the text. To
 locate them, the rows of the suffixes that start at a multiple of
 \f$ \mu \f$ keep their places: from any row, fewer than \f$ \mu \f$ steps
 of (12) reach a marked row, each step moving one place to the left in
 \f$ s \f$, and the place is that of the marked row plus the steps. The
 indexes of the area differ in how they compute the ranks of (13) and where
 they keep the samples.

 <table>
 <caption>The indexes of the area on a text of \f$ n \f$ bytes, a pattern
          of \f$ p \f$ symbols with \f$ \mathit{occ} \f$ occurrences</caption>
 <tr><th>Index</th><th>Space</th><th><tt>count( P )</tt></th>
     <th><tt>locate( P )</tt>, beyond the count</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>FMIndex</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ 8 n + 32 \sigma n / b \f$ bits, and the samples</td>
     <td>\f$ O( p b ) \f$, a rank a count every \f$ b \f$ places and a
         scan</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \mathit{occ} \, \mu b ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>HuffmanFMIndex</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ n ( H_0 + 1 ) ( 1 + o( 1 ) ) \f$ bits, and the samples</td>
     <td>\f$ O( p ( H_0 + 1 ) ) \f$ on average, a descent of the
         tree</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \mathit{occ} \, \mu ( H_0 + 1 ) ) \f$ on average</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RIndex</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( r ) \f$ words</td>
     <td>\f$ O( p ) \f$ ranks on the runs, each with a predecessor
         search</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \mathit{occ} ) \f$ predecessor searches</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MoveRIndex</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( r ) \f$ words, a few more per run</td>
     <td>\f$ O( p ) \f$ steps of \f$ O( 1 ) \f$, plus the ranks of the
         intervals of another symbol</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \mathit{occ} ) \f$, each in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 The samples every \f$ \mu \f$ places cost \f$ O( ( n / \mu ) \log n ) \f$
 bits, which is far more than \f$ L \f$ on a repetitive text (the versions
 of a document, the genomes of a species), where the number \f$ r \f$ of the
 runs of \f$ L \f$ is far smaller than \f$ n \f$. The r-index of Gagie,
 Navarro and Prezza keeps instead \f$ O( r ) \f$ words, by two properties.
 First, along the backward search the place \f$ \mathit{SA}[ e - 1 ] \f$ of
 the last row of the range (the <em>toehold</em>) is kept: if
 \f$ L[ e - 1 ] = c \f$ the row moves by (12) and the place decreases by 1,
 and otherwise the new last row comes from the last \f$ c \f$ before
 \f$ e - 1 \f$, which ends a run, whose place is sampled. Second, the
 function \f$ \phi( \mathit{SA}[ i ] ) = \mathit{SA}[ i - 1 ] \f$, which
 lists the other occurrences from the toehold, shifts alike every stretch of
 places between two places of the starts of the runs:
 \f[
   \phi( x ) = \phi( y ) + ( x - y ) , \qquad
   y = \max\{ \mathit{SA}[ p_j ] : \mathit{SA}[ p_j ] \le x \} , \tag{14}
 \f]
 \f$ p_j \f$ the start of the run \f$ j \f$. Indeed, if the row \f$ i \f$
 of \f$ x = \mathit{SA}[ i ] \f$ starts no run, then
 \f$ L[ i - 1 ] = L[ i ] \f$, Lemma 5 maps the rows \f$ i - 1 \f$ and
 \f$ i \f$ to adjacent rows, those of \f$ \phi( x ) - 1 \f$ and of
 \f$ x - 1 \f$, and hence \f$ \phi( x - 1 ) = \phi( x ) - 1 \f$; the
 places \f$ x \f$ whose row starts a run are the \f$ \mathit{SA}[ p_j ] \f$,
 and (14) follows by induction on \f$ x - y \f$. Hence one
 predecessor search among the \f$ r \f$ places \f$ \mathit{SA}[ p_j ] \f$ in
 an Elias-Fano sequence gives each step of \f$ \phi \f$ (`RIndex.h`). Both
 (12) on the runs and (14) map each interval of a partition alike, and the
 move structures of Nishimoto and Tabei split the intervals so that the
 interval of the image is found from that of the argument in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 steps, which removes the predecessor searches at the price of a few words
 per run (`MoveRIndex.h`, after the move-r of Bertram, Fischer and Nalbach).

